---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "mre lite protein powder",
      "redcon1 mre lite protein powder",
      "coach prime mre lite",
      "mre lite protein powder review",
      "coach prime mre lite red velvet protein powder"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Protein Powder Picks",
    link: "/best-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">MRE Lite Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      Redcon1's MRE Lite is sold as a whey-free, whole-food protein made from beef, chicken, salmon and egg. Here is what the label actually says, what it costs per 25 g of protein, and how it differs from the original MRE.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#whats-in-it">What Is in MRE Lite?</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Is It Third-Party Tested?</a>
      <a href="#whey-dairy">Whey-Free vs Dairy-Free</a>
      <a href="#mre-vs-lite">MRE vs MRE Lite</a>
      <a href="#coach-prime">Coach Prime MRE Lite</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#buyers">Taste and Complaints</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          MRE Lite is a whey-free protein powder that gives about 24 g of protein and 120 to 130 calories per scoop, per retail labels at the time of writing, from a blend led by beef protein isolate. It is not dairy-free, because the label adds milk powder. Below we cover the protein sources, sweeteners, testing status, the Coach Prime flavor, the original MRE and cost per 25 g.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. MRE Lite Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            MRE Lite is a reasonable whey-free protein powder at an average price: about 24 g of protein in 120 to 130 calories per scoop, with 0 g of added sugar, per retail labels at the time of writing, for about $1.39 to $1.49 per 25 g of protein on Amazon. It is not a meat-only protein, it is not dairy-free, and Redcon1 makes no third-party testing claim for it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> whey gives you bloating, you want a thick, milkshake-style shake, and you are fine with sucralose. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you have a milk, egg or fish allergy, you compete in a drug-tested sport and need a certified product, or you want to know exactly how much of each protein source is in the scoop.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">MRE Lite by the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">24 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per scoop (retail label)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">130</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories, Vanilla Milkshake</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">0 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Added sugar</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$1.39-$1.49</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g on Amazon</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The product cards below cover two MRE Lite flavors, the original high-calorie MRE, and two alternatives: a cheaper beef isolate and a dairy-free egg white powder. For the wider market, see our <a href="/best-protein-powder/">best protein powder</a> rankings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. MRE Lite, the Original MRE and Two Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every card below has a live Amazon listing we matched by brand, product line and size. Prices are what we saw on Amazon on 16 November 2026 and change often, so check the listing before you buy. Cost per 25 g uses the protein and servings printed on the label or the Amazon title.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="whats-in-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is in Redcon1 MRE Lite Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            MRE Lite is a blend of six protein sources: beef protein isolate, pea protein, chicken protein, egg albumin, salmon protein and brown rice protein, per the Vanilla Milkshake retail label at the time of writing. Redcon1's own product page lists the same six, describing the blend as beef protein, salmon protein, chicken protein, egg protein, brown rice protein and pea protein. Its marketing calls this "whole food protein" and an "animal-based protein blend", but two of the six sources are plants.
          </p>

          <p>
            The blend is proprietary. The label gives the total protein, not the grams contributed by each source, so you cannot tell whether a scoop is mostly beef, mostly pea or an even split. Ingredients are listed in descending order by weight, and beef protein isolate comes first on the retail labels we found. One buyer review on Redcon1's site complains about exactly this, arguing the lack of a breakdown or amino acid profile makes it impossible to judge the protein quality. That is a fair criticism: beef protein isolate is often rich in collagen-type proteins, which are lower in leucine than whey or egg, and Redcon1 does not publish a leucine figure.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Calories, carbs, fat and sweeteners</h3>

          <p>
            Per the Vanilla Milkshake label, one 31.5 g scoop gives 130 calories, 2 g of fat, 4 g of carbohydrate, 2 g of total sugar, 0 g of added sugar, 24 g of protein and 95 mg of sodium. The Oatmeal Chocolate Chip label is a little leaner on paper, at 120 calories, 1.5 g of fat and 3 g of carbs in a 32.5 g scoop, but its cookie pieces add sugar, wheat flour, high fructose corn syrup and soy lecithin to the ingredient list. Amazon titles quote between 1 g and 4 g of carbs per serving depending on flavor, so expect some variation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every MRE Lite label we checked is sweetened with sucralose and acesulfame potassium. Other ingredients include milk powder (listed for texture), MCT oil powder made with nonfat dry milk, natural and artificial flavors, salt, cellulose gum, silica and maltodextrin. That is a typical sports-supplement label, not a clean one, so if you want a short ingredient list without artificial sweeteners, MRE Lite is the wrong product.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Per scoop (label)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Vanilla Milkshake</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Oatmeal Chocolate Chip</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Scoop size</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">31.5 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">32.5 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">130</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Carbohydrate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Total / added sugar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 g / 0 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 g / 0 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.5 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sweeteners</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose, acesulfame K</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose, acesulfame K</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">24 g or 26 g of protein?</h3>

          <p>
            Redcon1's main MRE Lite product page now says 26 g of protein per serving, while the retail labels we found on Vitacost, the Amazon-sold 20-serving version and Redcon1's own 20-serving listing all say 24 g. That suggests a recent formula or scoop change that has not reached every retailer. We use 24 g in our cost math because it is what most tubs on shelves show; if your label says 26 g, the cost per 25 g drops by about 8%.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is MRE Lite Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not as far as we can verify. Redcon1's MRE Lite product page makes no third-party testing, banned-substance or heavy-metal testing claim, and no Redcon1 product appeared in the NSF Certified for Sport product listing when we checked on 16 November 2026. Redcon1 does not mention Informed Sport or Informed Choice on the MRE Lite page either.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some supplement trust-score websites claim Redcon1 products carry Informed Sport certification, but those are aggregators, not the certifier, and other aggregators say the opposite. A certification only counts if the certifier's own database lists the product and the batch. Until Redcon1 publishes a certificate or the product shows up in a certifier's database, treat MRE Lite as untested by an independent program.
          </p>

          <p>
            That matters most for two groups. Athletes subject to drug testing (NCAA, military, professional leagues) should use a product certified by NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport, because an uncertified powder carries the risk of contamination with a banned substance. And anyone worried about heavy metals should know that no lead or arsenic results are published for MRE Lite. Our guide to <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powders without heavy metals</a> lists brands that do publish results.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">No certificate, no testing claim</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">We only call a protein powder third-party tested when the brand names the program or the certifier's database lists it. MRE Lite meets neither test today. If that changes, the label or Redcon1's site will say so.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Lack of certification does not mean a product is unsafe, and Redcon1 is a large, established brand sold through GNC, Walmart, Vitacost and Amazon. It simply means you are trusting the manufacturer's own quality control. For more on how to judge that, see <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">is protein powder safe</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="whey-dairy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is MRE Lite Whey-Free and Dairy-Free?
          </h2>

          <p>
            MRE Lite is whey-free but not dairy-free. Redcon1 says the formula has no whey protein and labels it lactose free, yet the retail ingredient list includes milk powder and an MCT oil powder made with nonfat dry milk, and the allergen statement reads "Contains: milk, egg, fish (salmon), tree nuts (coconut)." Flavors with cookie pieces add wheat and soy to that list.
          </p>

          <p>
            The difference matters. Lactose intolerance is a problem digesting milk sugar, and a small amount of milk powder in a scoop may not bother many people who react to a full whey concentrate shake. A milk allergy is an immune reaction to milk proteins, and even small amounts can cause a reaction. Anyone with a diagnosed milk allergy should not use MRE Lite, and should check with a doctor or allergist before trying any protein that lists milk.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you need a protein that is genuinely free of dairy, look at single-source options. Pure egg white powders and beef isolates without added milk solids are covered in our <a href="/best-egg-white-protein-powder/">best egg white protein powder</a> and <a href="/best-beef-protein-powder/">best beef protein powder</a> guides, and our <a href="/best-dairy-free-protein-powder/">dairy-free protein powder</a> page includes plant blends too.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fish and egg are the other allergens to note. Salmon protein is unusual in a protein powder, and many buyers do not expect it. People with a fish allergy, an egg allergy or a coconut sensitivity should skip MRE Lite. If you are pregnant, have kidney disease or take medication that affects protein or potassium intake, ask your doctor or a registered dietitian before adding any protein supplement.
          </p>
        </div>

